WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.216 --> 00:00:01.885
- Alright, what's up, guys. I'm Nasher here joined

00:00:01.885 --> 00:00:04.488
alongside Trevor Zegras from the Anaheim Ducks.

00:00:04.488 --> 00:00:06.139
It's been a crazy week for you.

00:00:06.139 --> 00:00:08.358
I want to get down to the beginning, right?

00:00:08.358 --> 00:00:10.978
You starting as a hockey player, have you always been

00:00:10.978 --> 00:00:13.163
kind of that creative player? Like, were there any guys

00:00:13.163 --> 00:00:14.632
in the league that you were looking up to?

00:00:14.632 --> 00:00:16.934
Pavel Datsyuk, any guys like that?

00:00:16.934 --> 00:00:19.003
- Yeah, I mean, I was a big Kaner fan.

00:00:19.003 --> 00:00:20.004
- OK

00:00:20.287 --> 00:00:22.607
- When I was younger, I love I love the way he played.

00:00:22.607 --> 00:00:25.643
Obviously, Datsyuk, Crosby, I think all those guys,

00:00:26.661 --> 00:00:28.646
I mean, I think it just just in general, I think I was

00:00:28.646 --> 00:00:32.233
just a hockey fan, so I loved watching everybody.

00:00:32.233 --> 00:00:34.185
But I definitely say Kaner was probably my favorite

00:00:34.185 --> 00:00:37.105
player growing up and all the crazy passes and spin

00:00:37.105 --> 00:00:38.957
moves that he did. I mean, I loved trying that in the

00:00:38.957 --> 00:00:41.894
driveway or minor hockey or at prep school and all

00:00:41.894 --> 00:00:43.195
that good stuff.

00:00:43.195 --> 00:00:44.963
- So I want to talk about the play that happened with you

00:00:44.963 --> 00:00:46.198
and Sonny Milano.

00:00:46.198 --> 00:00:49.034
Obviously, you took the hockey world by storm.

00:00:49.034 --> 00:00:51.437
Walk me through that play step-by-step.

00:00:51.437 --> 00:00:52.938
So you're sitting behind the net.

00:00:52.938 --> 00:00:54.924
Milano calls out for the "Michigan".

00:00:54.924 --> 00:00:55.925
What's going through your head?

00:00:56.993 --> 00:00:58.995
- Yeah. Well, I mean, at first I tried to kind of just

00:00:58.995 --> 00:01:01.097
tried to settle the puck down and see if anybody would

00:01:01.097 --> 00:01:04.017
kind of chase me behind the net. And once my guy, I

00:01:04.017 --> 00:01:06.987
guess it was the right side for me, kind of just decided

00:01:06.987 --> 00:01:08.872
to chill out on the post.

00:01:08.872 --> 00:01:11.875
Obviously, Sony was calling for the "Michigan", and I

00:01:11.875 --> 00:01:14.361
knew I couldn't couldn't do the actual "Michigan" because

00:01:14.361 --> 00:01:16.797
that guy was kind of chillin' on the post.

00:01:16.797 --> 00:01:19.733
So I just flipped it over the net and he was ready for

00:01:19.733 --> 00:01:20.968
it and he made a great play.

00:01:20.968 --> 00:01:23.354
So I mean, I feel like most of the credit should be going

00:01:23.354 --> 00:01:26.457
to him because without Sonny, I mean, that's just

00:01:26.457 --> 00:01:28.759
a just a it's a it's a no play, really.

00:01:28.759 --> 00:01:31.963
And for him to kind of put it in that top corner,

00:01:31.979 --> 00:01:34.249
was was definitely pretty special on his end.

00:01:34.249 --> 00:01:36.568
- So this is not something that you guys had like, worked

00:01:36.568 --> 00:01:38.053
on practice the night before?

00:01:38.069 --> 00:01:39.888
The flip play? Obviously, you're working on that

00:01:39.888 --> 00:01:42.808
"Michigan" and lacrossing it all day, but the flip play

00:01:42.808 --> 00:01:45.077
was not something you drew up, right?

00:01:45.077 --> 00:01:46.912
- No, we never even talked about that before.

00:01:46.912 --> 00:01:47.930
- Wow

00:01:47.930 --> 00:01:50.032
- That's why I was pretty shocked when it went in, I didn't

00:01:50.032 --> 00:01:51.917
really know what happened, because the guy kind of pushed

00:01:51.917 --> 00:01:54.169
me after I flipped it over the net, so I didn't really

00:01:54.169 --> 00:01:56.756
see it. So I was kind of just in shock like did we really

00:01:56.756 --> 00:01:57.323
just do that?

00:01:57.323 --> 00:01:59.625
- Dude, that's unreal. I think that makes the play even

00:01:59.625 --> 00:02:02.061
better. Do you have a target on your back when you're

00:02:02.061 --> 00:02:04.097
behind the net? Like, do you feel like the opponents kind

00:02:04.097 --> 00:02:05.932
of know that these crazy things are coming?

00:02:05.932 --> 00:02:07.917
Do you feel like they want to maybe hit you harder

00:02:07.917 --> 00:02:09.018
because of that or anything?

00:02:10.453 --> 00:02:12.806
- I don't know. But it was- the next game, we we were in

00:02:12.806 --> 00:02:15.225
Columbus and we played Columbus, and I got behind the net

00:02:15.225 --> 00:02:18.028
in the first period and I got two guys kind of just came

00:02:18.028 --> 00:02:21.415
right on me and pinned me up the boards so I was like

00:02:21.415 --> 00:02:23.817
maybe that's maybe that's the end of my run behind the net.

00:02:23.817 --> 00:02:25.686
- So let's talk about some of your other plays too,

00:02:25.686 --> 00:02:28.522
right? You try to go between your legs, didn't go in, you

00:02:28.522 --> 00:02:30.474
go back to the bench. You're a little bummed out.

00:02:30.474 --> 00:02:33.177
Are your your teammates a little rattled by that?

00:02:33.177 --> 00:02:35.179
Or they want you to go with a simple backhand?

00:02:35.179 --> 00:02:36.180
Like, what's that like?

00:02:36.681 --> 00:02:38.483
- No, they think it's awesome.

00:02:38.483 --> 00:02:40.935
For the most part. Nobody really kind of gets on me for

00:02:40.935 --> 00:02:42.987
doing that stuff. I don't know, like a lot of people

00:02:42.987 --> 00:02:46.107
like, they love to like comment or chime in to say like

00:02:46.107 --> 00:02:47.509
he gotta just go backhand there.

00:02:47.509 --> 00:02:49.528
But like to me, like, I just don't understand.

00:02:49.528 --> 00:02:51.613
Like, what's the difference between like flipping a

00:02:51.613 --> 00:02:53.332
backhand versus like flipping a forehand?

00:02:53.332 --> 00:02:56.151
And I think, honestly, I looked at it a couple of times,

00:02:56.151 --> 00:02:59.054
and I think it was just a a great save by, I think it was

00:02:59.054 --> 00:03:01.273
Robin Lehner on Vegas.

00:03:01.273 --> 00:03:03.776
He read it great. And he kind of stayed with me.

00:03:03.776 --> 00:03:06.596
But I thought, I guess I had enough speed that he kinda

00:03:06.596 --> 00:03:08.998
be following the puck and it would kind of just sneak

00:03:08.998 --> 00:03:12.118
in, I guess, on the far side, but he made a great save.

00:03:12.118 --> 00:03:14.337
- You're the definition of changing the game, right,

00:03:14.337 --> 00:03:16.106
people have to change the way that they play and you're

00:03:16.106 --> 00:03:19.242
making them do that. And speaking of changing the game,

00:03:19.259 --> 00:03:21.478
we couldn't have this talk without bringing this up.

00:03:21.478 --> 00:03:23.397
John Tortorella, his comments on you.

00:03:23.397 --> 00:03:24.681
I'm sure you saw them.

00:03:24.681 --> 00:03:26.149
What are your thoughts on what he had to say?

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:30.321
- Yeah. I mean, I thought it was definitely a little out

00:03:30.321 --> 00:03:33.224
of the ordinary because, weird, I always liked

00:03:33.224 --> 00:03:35.626
Torts, man. Like growing up, I was a big Ranger fan.

00:03:35.626 --> 00:03:38.563
Obviously, he coached the Rangers, so I was always

00:03:38.579 --> 00:03:41.215
a big Tortorella fan. I love kind of that he was a little

00:03:41.215 --> 00:03:44.335
bit outspoken and had his own opinion, and Sonny,

00:03:44.335 --> 00:03:47.005
Sonny played for him for five years and Sonny had nothing

00:03:47.005 --> 00:03:49.190
but great things to say about him.

00:03:49.190 --> 00:03:52.160
So I guess for him to kind of take

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:54.262
that goal. I don't know if he took it personal or

00:03:54.262 --> 00:03:57.199
whatnot, but it's definitely, definitely

00:03:57.199 --> 00:03:58.767
a little out of the ordinary, for sure.

00:03:58.767 --> 00:04:01.370
And I guess, like you said, I don't know, changing the

00:04:01.370 --> 00:04:04.156
game, man. Like I saw so many, so many awesome videos of

00:04:04.156 --> 00:04:07.009
little kids trying it or, I guess, whether it

00:04:07.009 --> 00:04:08.544
was college kids or whatnot.

00:04:08.544 --> 00:04:11.397
So I mean, to me personally, I took

00:04:11.397 --> 00:04:14.233
a lot of pride in I guess maybe trying to inspire

00:04:14.233 --> 00:04:16.302
somebody to pick up a hockey stick.

00:04:16.319 --> 00:04:18.254
But I guess if you wanted to take it as it was bad for

00:04:18.254 --> 00:04:20.590
the game, I guess that's just his opinion.

00:04:20.590 --> 00:04:22.692
- Yeah, I can really respect that answer. I don't think

00:04:22.709 --> 00:04:25.428
Torts meant any, you know, ill intent by that.

00:04:25.428 --> 00:04:25.795
- Yeah

00:04:25.795 --> 00:04:27.914
- He's just old school, you know, dump the puck low,
- Totally

00:04:27.914 --> 00:04:30.867
shoot low for rebounds. That's that's the kind of guy he is but

00:04:30.867 --> 00:04:31.435
- Yeah

00:04:31.435 --> 00:04:33.887
- I could say personally, I walked into my rink for a

00:04:33.887 --> 00:04:35.539
beer league game this week. No joke.

00:04:35.539 --> 00:04:37.825
I saw the guys flipping the puck over the net going for

00:04:37.825 --> 00:04:40.327
your play, so you inspire the youth around the country,

00:04:40.327 --> 00:04:43.014
man. I appreciate everything you're doin', changing the

00:04:43.014 --> 00:04:46.551
game of hockey and we appreciate you hopping on this interview.

00:04:46.551 --> 00:04:48.953
- Thanks, Nasher. I picked up some stuff watching you on

00:04:48.953 --> 00:04:49.954
YouTube, bro.

00:04:50.471 --> 00:04:52.490
- No, you did not.

00:04:52.490 --> 00:04:54.342
- I saw your, I saw your roller hockey highlights, bro.

00:04:54.342 --> 00:04:56.628
That's where my, where some of the between the legs comes from

00:04:56.628 --> 00:04:59.097
- You've got to be kidding me. I posted that and I

00:04:59.097 --> 00:05:01.166
thought I was just kind of making a joke, I didn't know

00:05:01.166 --> 00:05:03.285
you actually watched the video.

00:05:03.285 --> 00:05:05.270
- Yeah, of course, man.

00:05:05.270 --> 00:05:07.856
- Dude I appreciate the love. Thank you so much and best

00:05:07.856 --> 00:05:09.508
of luck going forward this year, man.

00:05:09.508 --> 00:05:10.876
Thanks, man. Appreciate it.

